\f1\fs22 Sarah cried out, rolled over and threw her hand at the bedhead in a way that caused splinter haemorrhages in her finger nails.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>She was at the wake-up-or-die end of a nightmare, which it not say it was night time. It wasn\'92t. It was mid-afternoon, but we\'92ll cop no judging looks for Sarah taking a nana nap even before she was either old or yet a mother. She was exhausted, so get over it. Exhausted mostly from the recurring nightmares, afternoon-mares and the occasional mare after falling asleep in the bath.\cf2 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>She was bored\'97although trapped was probably the more pressing condition\'97and perhaps her unstimulated mind needed to concoct internal horror movies to keep her wetware fit and healthy.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Like most times before, Sarah unconsciously ripped herself out of dream state by sitting bolt upright, roaring with terror and rage, and then gasping for breath. She clumsily grabbed a glass on her night table, gulped down its contents and nearly drowned on it. Kathy appeared at her door as she spluttered up the last of the water that went down the wrong way.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Another bad dream, Holy Momma?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah grimaced. \'91Please stop calling me that.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy smiled; she was already separating Sarah from the clutches of the nightmare.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Pregnancy really suits you,\'92 Kathy said.  \'91You are radiant!\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Ha ha,\'92 Sarah grumbled. Somehow the thought of being radiant\'97radiating like, well, radiation\'97disturbed her. It connected her to the nightmare. \'91You look fantastic, too,\'92 Sarah told Kathy sarcastically, then noticed, if anyone was glowing, it as Kathy. Every day she looked more vivacious, more beautiful. \'91What\'92s your secret?\'92, she asked and fretted, after all the sec they were enjoying, the source of Kathy\'92s vitality was the same as her own.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy plumped down on the bed and caressed Sarah\'92s cheek. \'91Having someone to care for.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah didn\'92t know if Kathy was being sweet or creepy. Sarah could be her love interest, or some kind of crutch. In matters of love, Sarah was always suspicious. How would love her now, anyway? She was carrying some Frankenstein\'92s baby.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah jolted as if struck from the inside. She put her hand to her belly. Kathy noticed and said, \'91It\'92s still to early for Little Clinton to be kicking.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I know,\'92 Sarah protested. But she couldn\'92t explain the sensation\'97no, the knowledge\'97that Little Clinton was sending her the nightmares as a means of communicating. Of exactly what he was communicating, Sarah was clueless.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy slid the beside table drawer open and withdrew the sphygmomanometer she kept there.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Blood pressure again?\'92 Sarah complained.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Look! Just let me care for you, OK?\'92 Kathy was increasingly testy as she cut down on her patching, in support of Sarah\'92s new-found sobriety. No one wanted a patched-up foetus.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah pouted and offered her arm for Kathy to wrap the sphygmo-thingy\'92s pressure cuff around. That done, Kathy pushed the start button and let it auto-inflate. Sarah winced as she wondered if it would ever stop constricting her arm. Then it beeped and released her from its python-grip. Kathy ripped the Velcro of the cuff apart and took the instrument, before Sarah could see the numbers.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91How is it?\'92 Sarah asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91High.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91How high?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy knitted her eyebrows at Sarah. \'91High.\'92 She turned the thing off, wrapped its cuff around and deposited it back in the drawer. She sat on the bed. \'91Same nightmare?\'92 she asked with more empathy and less strict nurse bedside manor.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah shrugged. \'91Well, no \'85 but the same feelings of being in a time loop I can\'92t escape. I\'92m scared and helpless. I can\'92t breathe or push the anxiety away. The same smell \'85\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Like lab coats drenched in fish soup?\'92 Kathy shuddered with disgust. \'91Yuck. Lab coats.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91This time I see a pattern \'85 it\'92s burned or etched into a window or something. And \'85\'92 Sarah shudders.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What?\'92 Kathy rested her hand on Sarah\'92s thigh.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah gulped and shook; she fought back tears, then blurted. \'91I\'92m terrified of it. I know that sounds stupid, but there\'92s something \'85 
\f2\i evil
\f1\i0  is not the right word, but it feels so old, I know whatever caused it doesn\'92t care about us.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Us?\'92 Kathy was wondering if Sarah meant them as a couple. Not that they were a couple, of course. Kathy was never down for any kind of dependency. Yet. 
\f2\i Damn
\f1\i0 . Kathy craved to patch up. Her fingernails itched.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Us. All of us, this world \'85 perhaps the whole universe.\'92 Sarah looked like she\'92d met her own ghost. \'91There\'92s a kind of gravity to the pattern, like it wants to pull me in. The closer I look, the more detail resolves, like it\'92s never ending \'85\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91A fractal.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Yeah I suppose.\'92 Sarah grabbed Kathy\'92s shoulder, as if to steady herself. She gulped to fight back nausea. \'91It\'92s pulling me in and I can\'92t escape it.\'92 Her eyes glowed hot and, for the first time, Kathy feared for her. \'91It\'92s coming to get me and I can\'92t stop it. It\'92s coming to get us all.\'92
